{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788225663,"nonce":"MoZy/S5u7ms9ongkzpx46M3bQho=","title":"trade-set accepts two lines addressing the SAME Tradeable and echoes a deal state that never existed","message":"Found by the first live run of `accept/3.5-dialog-verbs.py --phase 3`, session 11 (`65b03c2`), 2026-08-31. Confirmed against the decompiled 1.6 source.\n\n\n`RimWorld/Tradeable.CountToTransfer` is a **single signed field**, and `Tradeable.ActionToDo` derives which side of the deal a row is on from that field's SIGN. There is one `Tradeable` per def. So \"buy 10 `ComponentIndustrial`\" and \"sell 5 `ComponentIndustrial`\" are not two lines — they are two writes to one number, and the last one wins.\n\n`trade-set` accepts both and echoes `accepted.0.count = +10` alongside `accepted.1.count = -5`. **That is a state the game never held.** What actually happened was `countToTransfer = -5`.\n\nMeasured live:\n\n- `lines_with_action == 2` — which is the components row plus the SILVER row, not two item rows. Two distinct item defs would have made it 3. That is the decisive tell.\n- Colony `ComponentIndustrial` went 50 → **45**. The sell applied; the buy did not.\n- Colony silver went 1600 → 1699, i.e. sell proceeds with no buy cost.\n- `data.after` is keyed by `ThingDef`, so the driver's `after_buy` and `after_sell` were the SAME OBJECT. One of the two checks reading it passed and the other failed off a single row.\n\n\nThis is the `4087644` defect class — an order reporting success for something that did not happen — on the trade surface. An agent running unattended reads `accepted` and believes the deal it asked for was struck. Over a ten-day run (`664e9b9`) that is a colony making decisions on a false ledger, and nothing in the envelope contradicts it.\n\n\nReject the second line at a `duplicate-line` gate: two `items` entries resolving to the same `Tradeable` cannot both be honoured, so the call should refuse rather than silently collapse. Refusing is right rather than netting them, because the caller asking for both almost certainly did not mean the net.\n\n\n- `Source/AutoRimmer/TradeVerbs.cs` — `TradeSet`, its gate chain and its `accepted` echo.\n- `RimWorld/Tradeable` — `CountToTransfer`, `ActionToDo`, `AdjustTo`.\n- `RimWorld/TradeDeal` — how rows are enumerated.\n\n\n- Two `items` entries resolving to the same `Tradeable` are refused, with the later line carrying a `duplicate-line` gate naming the def they collide on.\n- The refusal is journaled per the existing rule, and a wholly-refused call still writes no `journal_seq` (`PawnActs.NoStamp()`), matching what `TradeSet` already does.\n- A live acceptance check drives buy-and-sell of the SAME def and asserts the refusal — and separately drives buy and sell of TWO DISTINCT defs and asserts `lines_with_action == 3` (two item rows plus silver), so a collapse cannot hide behind a passing count again.\n- Zero red errors.","files":null}]}